The '@' markers are there for you to fill in the details based on the actual logic of the Remove method. The '@@@' markers indicate where a code assertion should be placed for that property tested. An example Assert is included.

[PexObserve.ValueForViewing](https://rehansaeed.com/introduction-to-pex-mutation-testing/) is used in Pex to log the value for viewing. [PexAssert](https://rehansaeed.com/introduction-to-pex-mutation-testing/) is another tool in Pex used to compare expected value with actual value and if it doesn't match, then the test fails.

Pex is powerful for mutation testing. Mutation testing is a method that attempts to evaluate how well your tests are protecting against changes in your code. This is especially valuable when dealing with larger existing codebases. The idea is that we introduce small, intentionally "bad" changes to the original software and see if our tests can catch them.

In this test, Pex will introduce mutations in the Remove function and check if our test cases are able to detect those mutations. The mutations should be relatively easy to detect as we are testing a basic method that simply removes a value from the list.

Pex supports multiple mutation operators such as Insert, Remove, Replace, Scramble, Invert, and Swap. These operators basically act like the equivalent of 'undo' command or reverts previous changes made by the mutation.

Pex testing is a tool that allows developers to easily apply many mutation operators on their test source code and analyze the effectiveness of test cases in detecting these mutations.
