A: In addition to the suggestions in previous answers (e.g., using RemoveAll instead of Remove, using PexModelStrongName, and using a wrapper method for removing elements), here are some additional pointers which could help in your situation:

1. Add custom action (i.e., a [PexCustomData] action) to specify that the method under test modifies a list that is assumed to be unchanged outside of it. In this scenario, you need to mark the List<T> argument as [PexAssumeNotNull]. You can read more about it in my previous answer. 

2. If you have a custom comparer or if your type does not override Equals and GetHashCode methods, then your tests might not execute correctly, even with [PexMethod] attribute applied. In this case Pex generates a test that attempts to add and then remove an item using the equality logic of your comparer, which might not work as you expect. To rectify this, consider using a wrapper method that accepts a parameter of a type that you control, does the comparison, and then passes the comparison result to the method under test.

3. If your list under test is large and performance might affect the results, consider using the [PexInlineData] attribute over the test method as shown below:

 ```
  [PexMethod]
  [PexInlineData(1)]
  [PexInlineData(2)]
  [PexInlineData(3)]
  public void RemoveTest(int countInList)
  {
     //..
  }
 ```
This will allow you to isolate the performance impact of your code under testing.

Hope these suggestions help you in your situation. Please let me know if you need further assistance.

Update after further consideration: The performance and results would significantly depend on the type and nature of data in your list. If your list has very large data (thousands of items or more), then testing might not be optimal. You should try to test with a mix of different data sizes and scenarios to ensure your tests cover most possible edge cases for your specific use case. 

Again, note that the specific details would depend on the method under test and how it interacts with your data. Tests should be designed and customised according to these specific requirements.

Also, keep in mind that mutation testing (e.g., unit tests) should be used alongside property-based testing (e.g., Pex) for comprehensive testing. For instance, in your specific test case, you might want to create additional tests to confirm whether the list behavior is as expected when a large number of items are added, removed or when the list is filled beyond the maximum allowed size.

Overall, the overall coverage will vary depending on the specific requirements of your method under test and whether or not your understanding of your application's behavior aligns with the automated test's behavior.

Happy Testing!!!


Here is some code to demonstrate using attributes:

```
public void MyRemoveContract([PexMethod] [PexAssumeNotNull] [PexInlineData(1)] List<int> list, [PexAssumeValid] int num) {

   //..

}
```
Remember that Pex will modify these and many other aspects of your code, so your test might fail, and that’s expected. Your job is to identify why your test fails, not to get it to pass by accident.


Update 2:

You can also consider using the FsCheck, a property-based testing library that helps in verifying the properties of your method under tests. This is particularly helpful when you have complex, stateful systems to test.


Update 3:

