# Narrowing down to a specific contract

After running the test, it becomes clear that the `PUT_Remove` method has failed with the error "`ArrayList.Test.PUT_RemoveContract (Contract_003)`: There was a contract execution error: PutsContract/Remove(ArrayList, int32) did not produce an outcome."

Next step is to narrow down to the specific contract `Remove(ArrayList, int32)`. The failure happened due to "PutsContract/Remove", which is incorrect. This signifies that we are only testing remove method but we should test clear method too.

So, the next step is to revise the tests to ensure both the remove and clear methods pass. This is achieved by writing separate contract methods for each method to be contract tested.

# Summary

This experiment has demonstrated the use of `Contract.Result` to generate random data that passes the specified contracts and verify the test case. The `Contract.Result` is a useful tool in fuzz testing that helps us generate various types of edge and corner cases. It helps us to make assertions that our code behaves correctly for various inputs and scenarios. Contract testing helps improve the robust, maintainable and usable of the code being tested. It tests methods that are expected to have preconditions, postconditions and effects on the behavior of the system around the method. A good practice is to divide the contract methods into the edge cases, normal cases and boundaries which are commonly known as "Smoke Tests".

